        -:    8:/**
        -:    9: * @file shot_detect.c
        -:   10: * @brief Variant-V4 archery release detector — implementation.
        -:   11: *
        -:   12: * Two parallel trigger channels run on each 200 Hz IMU sample:
        -:   13: *
        -:   14: *   accel:  |a|      -> highpass(10 Hz) -> square -> mean(25 ms) -> sqrt = E
        -:   15: *   gyro:   |omega|                     -> square -> mean(25 ms) -> sqrt = G
        -:   16: *
        -:   17: * E is the high-frequency hand-shock envelope; G is the bow-rotation
        -:   18: * envelope. Each has its own adaptive baseline (an EMA taken while the
        -:   19: * bow is held), and a release is triggered when EITHER channel crosses
        -:   20: * its baseline-scaled threshold — a heavily damped compound with a weak
        -:   21: * hand-shock still snaps into a fast forward rotation and is caught on G.
        -:   22: * A post-onset window then confirms the release by its ring-down (on
        -:   23: * either channel). A small FSM — IDLE -> HOLD -> VALIDATING ->
        -:   24: * REFRACTORY — sequences it.
        -:   25: *
        -:   26: * FSM durations (hold, settle, validation window, refractory) are
        -:   27: * counted in SAMPLES, not wall-clock time: imu_task stamps each sample
        -:   28: * at processing time, so a preempted task or batched IMU reports would
        -:   29: * bunch the timestamps. One tick is one sample regardless, so sample
        -:   30: * counts are immune to that. Wall-clock (`t_us`) is used only to form
        -:   31: * the reported onset timestamp.
        -:   32: *
        -:   33: * Constants here are starting points; the floor values and the K
        -:   34: * multipliers are meant to be tuned from real-bow envelope recordings —
        -:   35: * the 1 Hz diagnostic log exists for exactly that.
        -:   36: */
        -:   37:
